# This unit's real hardware configuration — the Mac Studio XLeRobot.
#
#   set -a && source deploy/mac/xlerobot.env.example && set +a
#   python3 sidecar/xlerobot_sidecar.py        # then open http://127.0.0.1:8900/control
#
# Every value below was established empirically against this specific robot
# (see SIDECAR.md's servo-bus-layout note and lex-robot#146). Copy and edit for
# another unit — do NOT assume these transfer. Serial device names in
# particular are per-host: on Linux the same arms appear as /dev/ttyACM*.

LEX_ROBOT_HW=1
LEX_ROBOT_SIDECAR_PORT=8900

# ── arms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CORRECTED 2026-08-29. These two were the wrong way round.
#
# The old assignment came from a gripper heuristic — "the arm whose gripper was
# open is the right one, viewed from behind" — and that heuristic was simply
# wrong. Replaced with a direct observation that cannot be misread: shoulder_pan
# on 5B3D0437151 was swept +/-320 ticks six times and a human watched which
# physical arm moved. It is the RIGHT arm. That also agrees with the wheels
# being on it (see the base section) on a robot whose wheels hang off the right
# arm's bus.
#
# Device names follow the USB-serial adapters' own serial numbers, so they
# survive replug — it is only the left/right LABELS that were inverted, no
# cable was moved.
LEX_XLE_LEFT_PORT=/dev/cu.usbmodem5B610332201
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_PORT=/dev/cu.usbmodem5B3D0437151

# Must match the calibration files in
# ~/.cache/huggingface/lerobot/calibration/robots/so_follower/<id>.json.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE "FIXING" THE APPARENT MISMATCH: the profile NAMES are
# historical and now sit opposite their sides. `xle_left.json` is the profile
# of the arm on 5B3D0437151, which the line above establishes is the RIGHT arm.
# The names are just lookup keys — what matters is that each port is given the
# profile matching ITS OWN motor EEPROM, and that is what these two lines do.
# Verified 2026-08-29 on Min/Max_Position_Limit: 5B3D0437151 matches xle_left
# 6/6 and xle_right 0/6; 5B610332201 matches xle_right 6/6 and xle_left 0/6.
# Renaming the two JSON files would read better but would diverge from the
# pi-env-camera-map branch's calibration/ copies, which use these same names.
LEX_XLE_LEFT_ID=xle_right
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_ID=xle_left

# NOTE: this unit needs no interactive calibration — the arms' motor EEPROM
# still carries it, and `bus.read_calibration()` recovers it into those files.
# Resynced from EEPROM 2026-08-29 after xle_right.json was found stale (it held
# pre-recalibration ranges from before lex-robot#192 rewrote the EEPROM).

# Caps each commanded step. Keep it: a second line of defence under whatever
# envelope the caller enforces.
LEX_XLE_MAX_REL_TARGET=8

# ── cameras ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Index -> physical camera was established by jogging each arm and diffing
# frames; each wrist camera responds ~10x more to its own arm. Do NOT infer
# these from enumeration order — they have been wrong before.
#
# ALSO CORRECTED 2026-08-29, and for the same reason as the arm ports. The
# index->ADAPTER binding was measured and stands: index 2 moves with the arm on
# 5B3D0437151, index 1 with 5B610332201. Only the left/right label was wrong,
# and it inverts with the arms above — 5B3D0437151 is the RIGHT arm, so index 2
# is the RIGHT wrist camera.
#
# RE-MEASURED 2026-08-29 and confirmed. Method: open both wrist cameras, sweep
# ONE gripper ~900 ticks, diff the frames — but first measure a no-motion noise
# floor, because without it auto-exposure drift swamps the signal (a first run
# without the noise floor made cam2 look like it belonged to BOTH arms). With
# it the separation is total:
#     left gripper moves  -> cam1 15.0x noise, cam2 0.9x  (i.e. cam2 unmoved)
#     right gripper moves -> cam2  8.2x noise, cam1 1.1x  (i.e. cam1 unmoved)
#
# macOS TCC note for whoever runs this next: a Claude Code session inside the
# Claude desktop app CANNOT open these cameras, and "Claude" never appears in
# System Settings > Privacy & Security > Camera to be granted. Terminal.app
# already holds the grant, so run camera work from there — e.g.
#   osascript -e 'tell application \"Terminal\" to do script \"...\"'
# Servo buses are unaffected; only the cameras are TCC-gated.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEAD_INDEX=0
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_LEFT_INDEX=1
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_RIGHT_INDEX=2

# REQUIRED on this unit, not an optimisation. These UVC cameras negotiate
# 1920x1080 by default and the USB topology cannot carry three at once: held
# open together at 1080p the third returns NO frame and renders as "camera
# unavailable". At 640x480 all three stream concurrently. Anything reading two
# views at once silently loses one without this.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_WIDTH=640
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEIGHT=480

# This unit calibrates the gripper the OPPOSITE way to the sidecar's default:
# 0 is CLOSED and 100 is OPEN (verified on hardware -- raw 2048 = fingers
# touching, raw 3453 = wide). Without these, release_arm drives the gripper
# further shut while reporting success.
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_OPEN_PCT=100
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_CLOSED_PCT=0

# ── kinematics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# move_arm's Cartesian IK/FK needs an on-disk URDF plus placo
# (pip install "lerobot[kinematics]"). Sparse-clone per SIDECAR.md; kept out of
# /tmp so it survives a reboot.
LEX_XLE_URDF_PATH=${HOME}/.local/share/so-arm100/Simulation/SO101/so101_new_calib.urdf
LEX_XLE_URDF_TARGET_FRAME=gripper_frame_link

# ── base ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The wheels share an arm's physical bus rather than having their own port, so
# the base reuses that arm's connected bus (lex-robot#145).
#
# Re-measured 2026-08-29. The value is `right` — the same as it always said —
# but it had been reached by assumption, and for a while during this session it
# was wrongly "corrected" to `left`. It is right for a reason now:
#
#   1. Ping sweep of ids 1-20 per adapter, which trusts no naming at all:
#        5B3D0437151 answers 1-6 + 9,10
#        5B610332201 answers 1-6 + 7,8
#   2. Operating_Mode of those auxiliaries, which tells the two kinds of device
#      apart regardless of what id they carry:
#        5B3D0437151 ids 9,10 -> mode 1, VELOCITY  = wheels
#        5B610332201 ids 7,8  -> mode 0, position  = pan/tilt tower
#   3. A human watched 5B3D0437151's shoulder_pan sweep and named it the RIGHT
#      arm (see the arms section).
#
# So: wheels on 5B3D0437151 = the right arm. The pan/tilt tower is on the LEFT
# arm's bus, which matters for the scripts carried over from lex-robot#175 —
# scripts/measure_camera_fov.py takes the tower's bus from LEX_XLE_LEFT_PORT,
# which is now correct here only because the arm labels were fixed above.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-29 — this was the long-standing UNVERIFIED note, now closed.
# Each wheel was spun on its own while a human watched: id 9 is the LEFT wheel,
# id 10 the RIGHT, viewed from behind. So the two lines below are correct.
LEX_XLE_BASE=diff
LEX_XLE_BASE_SHARED_ARM=right
LEX_XLE_BASE_LEFT_ID=9
LEX_XLE_BASE_RIGHT_ID=10

# Wheel mounting polarity. The wheels on this unit are MIRROR MOUNTED, so the
# same signed command turns them opposite ways in the body frame: driven both
# positive the robot pivots on the spot instead of driving. Measured on the
# floor 2026-08-29 — left(9)=-90 right(10)=+90 travels FORWARD, the inverse
# travels backward — so the left wheel is inverted here.
#
# NOTE the old advice in this file was "if a forward command turns the robot,
# swap LEX_XLE_BASE_LEFT_ID and _RIGHT_ID". That CANNOT work and has been
# removed: a forward command sends both wheels an identical value, so swapping
# which id is called 'left' produces the same bus write byte for byte. Swapping
# the ids fixes a TURN that goes the wrong way; only a sign fixes a forward
# that pivots. Pinned by test_swapping_the_ids_cannot_fix_a_forward_command_
# that_pivots in sidecar/test_xlerobot_hw.py.
LEX_XLE_BASE_LEFT_SIGN=-1
LEX_XLE_BASE_RIGHT_SIGN=1

# ── not set, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LEX_XLE_GRANT_PATH   defaults to manifests/xlerobot.capsule.json
# LEX_XLE_VISION_URL   set when running sidecar/vision_service.py
# macOS note: anything opening a camera must be launched from a bundled app
# (Terminal.app). TCC attributes the request to the responsible process, and a
# bare python deep in a shell chain is denied silently, with no entry ever
# appearing under System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Camera.
